        ":question/id": "4cc4c9bb-792c-5a39-b8cc-6ae72f7c3728",
        ":question/difficulty": ":question.difficulty/easy",
        ":question/problem": "$\\int \\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x=$",
        ":question/worked-solution": "Let $u=\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right).$ Differentiating, we get\n\n$$\n\\frac{\\text{d}u}{\\text{d}x}=\\frac{1}{x \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}}\\,\\Longrightarrow\\,\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\,\\text{d}u=\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x}.\n$$\n\nUsing the above, we can write the integral in terms of $u$ and evaluate:\n\n$$\n\\begin{aligned}\\int \\frac{\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)}{x}\\,\\text{d}x & =1\\int \\log_{2}\\left(x\\right)\\cdot\\frac{\\text{d}x}{x} \\\\  & =1\\int u\\cdot \\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\int u\\,\\text{d}u \\\\  & =\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)}\\cdot \\frac{u^{2}}{2}+C \\\\  & =\\frac{\\ln{\\left(2 \\right)} (\\log_{2}\\left(x\\right))^{2}}{2}+C\\end{aligned}\n$$",
